'''Classical Mongolian''' was the literary language of [[Mongolian language|Mongolian]] that was first introduced shortly after 1600 when [[Ligdan Khan]] set his clergy the task of translating the whole of the [[Tibetan Buddhist canon]], consisting of the [[Kangyur]] and [[Tengyur]], into Mongolian.{{sfn|Grønbech|Krueger|1993|pp=5, 7}} This script then became the established writing system used for all Mongolian literature until the 1930s when the [[Mongolian Latin alphabet]] was introduced, which then in 1941 was replaced by the [[Mongolian Cyrillic alphabet]].{{sfn|Grønbech|Krueger|1993|p=8}} 

Classical Mongolian was formerly used in [[Mongolia]], China, and Russia. It is a standardized written language used in the 18th century and 20th centuries.{{sfn|Janhunen|2003|p=32}}

''Classical Mongolian'' sometimes refers to any language documents in [[Mongolian script]] that are neither Pre-classical (i.e. [[Middle Mongol]] in the [[Mongolian script]]) nor [[Mongolian language|modern Mongolian]].<ref>e.g. [http://linguistlist.org/forms/langs/LLDescription.cfm?code=cmg Linguist List entry for Classical Mongolian] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120218072717/http://linguistlist.org/forms/langs/LLDescription.cfm?code=cmg |date=2012-02-18 }}</ref>

